<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Committee of Togolese Unity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Comité de l'unité togolaise</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Committee of Togolese Unity was a political party in French Togoland, founded in 1941 as a cultural association and turned into a nationalist party after 1946 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylvanus-olympio">Sylvanus Olympio</a>. It petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> for independence and for the unification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewe-people">Ewe people</a> divided between British and French Togoland. It won the UN-supervised elections of 1958, and Olympio led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> to independence in 1960.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/committee-of-togolese-unity.jpg" alt="Committee of Togolese Unity" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edem Fiadjoe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armoiries_du_Togo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
